Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[{
  "Week 13   Projective Geometry\n",
  StyleBox["Classification of Cubics",
    FontSize->18]
}], "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["\[Copyright] Dana Scott 1993/1997/1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  StyleBox["\nNotebook  last edited: ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["30 November 1998",
    FontSize->16,
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Subtitle",
  FontSize->18],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Classifying Singular Cubics", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A normal form for cuspidal cubics", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Projective equivalence of cuspidal cubics", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
Curves can look very different when we graph them, but projective \
transformations (especially those using complex numbers) can show that curves \
are in fact really the same under a change of coordinate systems.  We treat \
the cubics with a cusp first.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  All cuspidal cubics are projectively equivalent."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We know that a cuspidal cubic has ",
  StyleBox["one cusp",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (with a double tangent) and (at least) ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["inflection point ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "(distinct from the cusp).  Consider such a curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  By a projective transformation, we can move the cusp to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and the inflection to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Now, consider the ",
  StyleBox["two tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " at these ",
  StyleBox["two",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points.  These have to be ",
  StyleBox["distinct lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  So we can make another projective transformation leaving ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " fixed and moving the ",
  StyleBox["intersection of the tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Any point on the double tangent to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", the cusp,",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "has to be a ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the conic polar, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, w]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Thus, we find ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, w\ u] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Also, the tangent to the curve at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a factor of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f, v]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", so ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on this locus as well.  These considerations give us the following \
equations about the three points as related to the curve.  We enter them as \
declarations about the curve and these significant points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, u, v, w]; \)\), 
    \(f /: DegP[f] = 3; \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 
    Nil["\<f is a cubic\>"]; \n\(\((w /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, w\^3]]; \ \ \ \ 
    Nil["\<w is on curve\>"]; \n\(\((w /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, u\ w\^2]]; \ 
    Nil["\<w is a double point\>"]; \n\(\((w /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, v\ w\^2]]; 
    \ \n\(\((v /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, v\^3]]; \ \ \ \ \ 
    Nil["\<v is on the curve\>"]; \n\(\((w /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, w\ u\^2]]; \ 
    Nil["\<u is on tangent at w\>"]; \n\(\((w /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, w\ u\ v]]; 
    Nil["\<u dble pt on tangent\>"]; \n\(\((v /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, v\^2\ u]]; 
    \ \ Nil["\<u is on tangent at v\>"]; \n
    \(\((v /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, v\ u\^2]]; \ \ 
    Nil["\<u is on conic polar\>"]; \)}], "Input",
  FontSize->15],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us now see how the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " behaves with respect to the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["10",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-monomials:"
}], "Text"],

Cell["PD[f,#]&/@Buvw[3]", "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Good!  All but 2 are zeroed out.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "   So, to reach a final form, the vectors ",
  StyleBox["u",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " can be altered by ",
  StyleBox["scalar factors",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " using a projective transformation in order to make the two non-zero \
values shown above ",
  StyleBox["anything we please",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (Note, complex numbers may be required for this passage.)"
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Let us see what we would actually like.  Since the third \
derivatives of the polynomial essentially give its coefficients, we should \
try a simple-looking cubic with just two terms:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(f = x\^3 - y\^2\ z; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["What does this do the the monomial basis?", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@Buvw[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Excellent!  We can make our given, arbitrary cuspidal cubic into ",
  StyleBox["this one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if we like.  We have thus shown that all these cubics are ",
  StyleBox["projectively equivalent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["Here, by the way is its degenerate Hessian:", "Text"],

Cell["h = Det[MHP[f]]", "Input"],

Cell["\<\
And the following computation proves there is only one inflection \
point.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[{f == 0, h == 0, x == 1}, {x, y, z}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[{f == 0, h == 0, y == 1}, {x, y, z}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[{f == 0, h == 0, z == 1}, {x, y, z}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here is a picture of the real part of these two curves.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, x == 0, y == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, {x, \(-1\), 
        3}, {y, \(-4\), 4}, Axes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.006], Black}, {Thickness[0.006], Red}, {
            Thickness[0.006], Red}}]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
We can also peek at part of an imaginary section of the curve.\
\>",
   "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[\((f /. {y \[Rule] I\ y, z \[Rule] 1})\) == 0, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 1}, {y, \(-4\), 4}, \n\tAxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}]; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can also bring the infinite points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " down where we can see them by a projective transformation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(mm = {{3, 0, 1}, {0, 2, 1}, {0, 0, 1\/10}}; \n\(TP[mm]\)[u]\), 
    \(\(TP[mm]\)[v]\), 
    \(\(TL[mm]\)[f] == 0\)}], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{\(TL[mm]\)[f] == 0, \(TL[mm]\)[x] == 0, \n\t\t
          \(TL[mm]\)[y] == 0, \(TL[mm]\)[PD[f, v\^2]] == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 5}, {y, \(-2\), 4}, \n\tAxes \[Rule] None, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 151, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.006], Black}, {Thickness[0.006], Red}, \n
          \t\t\t{Thickness[0.006], Red}, {Thickness[0.006], Blue}}]; \)\)], 
  "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The cuspidal cubic as an Abelian group", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Here is a ",
  StyleBox["parameterization",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the standard curve.  There are many excellent properties of this \
parameterization.  We note that all the points on the curve, save for the \
cusp ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", can be reached by it. (Why?)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f]; \nf = x\^3 - y\^2\ z; \nP[a_] := a\ u + v + a\^3\ w; \n
    PD[f, P[a]\^3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If we want to accomodate ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " we would have take ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == ComplexInfinity\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Obviously the parametrization is also ",
  StyleBox["one-to-one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " between all (finite) complex numbers and all points on the curve (except \
for the cusp)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can show that there is only one point (namely ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", given by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ") where the Hessian of the conic polar vanishes \[LongDash] just as we \
expected.  That means this is the only ",
  StyleBox["inflection point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We will call ",
  StyleBox["v",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " the ",
  StyleBox["zero point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the curve",
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Det[MHP[PD[f, P[a]]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Suppose ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[b]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[c]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are three points on the curve.  When are they ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?  Of course, the condition is the vanishing of the following:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[PE[P[a], P[b]], P[c]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So we see that the condition is that ",
  StyleBox["either",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " two of the points are ",
  StyleBox["equal",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["or",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " that  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a + b + c == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Suppose ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a\_1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a\_2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a\_3]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a\_4]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a\_5]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a\_6]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["six distinct points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the curve.  When do they all lie on a ",
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "?  The condition is that the following quantity should be zero: "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[P2P[P[a\_1], P[a\_2], P[a\_3], P[a\_4], P[a\_5]], P[a\_6]\^2]]
      \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Pretty amazing!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  The condition is that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_1 + a\_2 + a\_3 + a\_4 + a\_5 + a\_6 == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Hence, if three of these points are collinear, then so are the other \
three.  If no subtriple is collinear, this will be a non-degenerate conic."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The ",
  StyleBox["geometric meaning",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the parameterization can also be explained by taking lines from points \
on the cubic to the ",
  StyleBox["cusp",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[w, P[a]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, the point ",
  StyleBox["P[a]",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " makes a line with the origin of slope ",
  StyleBox["1/a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  The intersection of this line with the line ",
  StyleBox["y == 1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is at the point with Cartesian coordinates ",
  StyleBox["{a, 1}",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PE[PE[w, P[a]], z - y]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
So that translates the points on the cubic to points on a line with \
the parameterizations closely related.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem. ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Excluding the cusp, all the other points on the cuspidal cubic form a ",
  StyleBox["group",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " isomorphic to the ",
  StyleBox["additive group",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["of the complex numbers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The inflection point plays the role of the ",
  StyleBox["zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the group."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "Let ",
  StyleBox["f",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " be any cuspidal cubic, and let ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " be the inflection point.  The ",
  StyleBox["additive group operation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " can be defined by carrying out the following steps."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t",
  StyleBox["(1) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Take two points on the curve and join them by a line.  \n\t",
  StyleBox["(2) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Find the third intersection of this line with the cubic.  \n\t",
  StyleBox["(3) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Join this point with the zero point.  \n\t",
  StyleBox["(4) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Find the third intersection of this second line with the curve.  \n\t",
  StyleBox["(5) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "That unique point is the answer, called the ",
  StyleBox["sum",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the two points."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0.2]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In step ",
  StyleBox["(1)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we have to use the tangent line to the cubic in case the two given \
points are equal.  In the case of the inflection point ",
  StyleBox["n ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "itself, all the lines are the inflectional tangent, and so the sum of ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " with itself is again ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Thus, it is a ",
  StyleBox["netural element",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is also easily seen that the sum with ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and any other point ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " on the curve is ",
  StyleBox["p",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " \[LongDash] so ",
  StyleBox["n",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is a ",
  StyleBox["zero element",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as well.   "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The other group properties are not so easy to see, and we resort to the \
normal form and the parametric representation of the curve.  Note that the \
construction of the group operation is given entirely in ",
  StyleBox["projective terms",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and so is completely ",
  StyleBox["invariant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " under projective transformations.  So that means there is ",
  StyleBox["no loss of generality",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " in going to the canonical form for the cubic."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "What we found in specifying the parametrization ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(P[a]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is that \n\t",
  StyleBox["(i) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "it gave in a one-one fashion all points on the curve save for the ",
  StyleBox["cusp",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", \n\t",
  StyleBox["(ii)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " the ",
  StyleBox["inflection point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " was ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(P[0]\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", and \n\t",
  StyleBox["(iii)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " three points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a\_1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a\_2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a\_3]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "on the curve were ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if, \t       and only if,  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox[\(a\_1\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["+",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], \(a\_2\), 
          StyleBox["+",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[" ",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[\(a\_3\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["0",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  \nHence, steps ",
  StyleBox["(1) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and ",
  StyleBox["(2)  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "will take us from points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a\_1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a\_2]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "on the curve to point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"P", "[", 
          RowBox[{
            StyleBox[\(-a\_1\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["-",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], \(a\_2\)}], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "on the curve.  Then steps ",
  StyleBox["(3) ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and",
  StyleBox[" (4)",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " will take us from points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[0]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "and  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"P", "[", 
          RowBox[{
            StyleBox[\(-a\_1\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["-",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], \(a\_2\)}], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "on the curve to the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"P", "[", 
          RowBox[{
            StyleBox[\(a\_1\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "+", 
            StyleBox[\(a\_2\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "on the curve.  Since the correspondence is one to one, this is an ",
  StyleBox["isomorphism",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with the additive group of complex numbers.  Therefore, the geometric \
construction gives an operation satisfying the group properties.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that the additive group of complex numbers is ",
  StyleBox["torsion free ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "(",
  StyleBox["i.e.",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", whenever ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(k\ x == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[",",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " then ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ").  This property (for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(k == 3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ") is related to the fact that the cuspidal cubic can have ",
  StyleBox["only one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " inflection point. "
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["A normal form for nodal cubics", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Projective equivalence of nodal cubics", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["Here is the second theorem to be proved.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  All nodal cubics are projectively equivalent."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "A nodal cubic has ",
  StyleBox["one node",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", which is a double point with distinct tangents.  Consider such a curve ",
  
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By a projective transformation, we can move the node to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Now consider the two tangents at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  These have to be distinct lines.  So we can make another projective \
transformation bringing ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to these two tangents, one on each.  These considerations give us the \
following five equations about these points as related to the curve:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[f, u, v, w]; \)\), 
    \(f /: DegP[f] = 3; \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 
    Nil["\<f is a cubic\>"]; \n\(\((w /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, w\^3]]; \ \ \ 
    Nil["\<w is on the cubic\>"]; \n\(\((w /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, u\ w\^2]]; 
    Nil["\<w will be a dble pt\>"]; \n\(\((w /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, v\ w\^2]]; 
    Nil["\<ditto\>"]; \n\(\((w /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, w\ u\^2]]; 
    Nil["\<u on the conic polar\>"]; \n\(\((w /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, w\ v\^2]]; 
    Nil["\<v on the conic polar\>"]; \)}], "Input",
  FontSize->15],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let us now see how the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " behaves with respect to the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["10",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["uvw",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-monomials:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@Buvw[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Unfortunately, this does not as yet give ",
  StyleBox["enough zeros",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Note that the results above would not change if we replaced ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by other points on the two tangents at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Thus, consider replacing ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u\  + \ a\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(v + b\ w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, \((v + b\ w)\) \((u + a\ w)\)\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[f, \((u + a\ w)\) \((v + b\ w)\)\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Clearly, then, there would be values of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["a",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " that would make these zero.  Thus, ",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " projective transformations we can assume these additional equations:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(\((v /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, v\ u\^2]]; \ \ 
    Nil["\<v on tangent at u\>"]; \n\(\((v /: # = 0)\)&\)[PD[f, u\ v\^2]]; 
    \ \ Nil["\<u is on tangent v\>"]; \)], "Input"],

Cell["Now, let us see how things look:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #]&)\)/@Buvw[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Good!  All but 3 are now zeroed out.   So, as a ",
  StyleBox["final step",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we remark that the vectors ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " can be altered by scalar factors to make the three non-zero values \
anything we please.  Let us see what we would like have best.  Here is the \
obvious kind of cubic:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(f = x\^3 + y\^3 - 6\ x\ y\ z; \)\), 
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-4\), 4}, {y, \(-4\), 4}, \n\tAxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 70, PlotStyle \[Rule] {Thickness[0.006]}]; \)\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell["What does this do the the monomial basis?", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f, #1]&)\)/@Buvw[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Excellent!  We can make our given, arbitrary nodal cubic into ",
  StyleBox["this one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if we like.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Here, by the way is its Hessian (with a numerical factor \
eliminated):\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h = Det[\(-\(MHP[f]\/6\)\)]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
This is a similar curve sharing the same double point and with the \
same tangents there:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{f == 0, h == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-4\), 4}, {y, \(-4\), 4}, \n\tPlotPoints \[Rule] 100, \n\t
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.006], Black}, {Thickness[0.006], Red}}]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can see that ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is indeed a double point of both the curve and the Hessian."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({PD[f, w], PD[h, w]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The most obvious inflection point is  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u - v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " with tangent line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x + y + 2\ z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({PD[f, \((u - v)\)\^3], PD[h, \((u - v)\)\^3]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({Factor[PD[f, u - v]], Factor[PD[h, u - v]]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The other inflection points use the other complex cube roots of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(-1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  They also lie on the line at infinity."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(TableForm[Solve[{f == 0, h == 0} /. x \[Rule] 1, {y, z}]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["We can write these easier in a symbolic form.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(a = \((\(-1\))\)\^\(1/3\); \)\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Of course we know  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == 1\/2\ \((1 + I\ \@3)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Here are the three inflection points and the double points of the \
conic polars.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({p\_1, p\_2, p\_3} = {u - v, u + a\ v, a\ u + v}; {r\_1, r\_2, r\_3} = {
        u + v - w, a\^2\ u + v + a\ w, u + a\^2\ v + a\ w}; \n\n
    \((PD[f, #1\^3]&)\)/@{p\_1, p\_2, p\_3} == 
      \((PD[h, #1\^3]&)\)/@{r\_1, r\_2, r\_3} == 
      ExpandAll[{PD[f, p\_1\ r\_1], PD[f, p\_2\ r\_2], 
          PD[f, p\_3\ r\_3]}] == {0, 0, 0}\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The three inflection points are collinear, and the line joining two \
double points always passes through one of the inflection points.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Det[MP[{p\_1, p\_2, p\_3}]] == \n\tDet[MP[{p\_1, r\_2, r\_3}]] == \n\t
      Det[MP[{p\_2, r\_1, r\_3}]] == \n\tDet[MP[{p\_3, r\_1, r\_2}]] == 0\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The only problem with these nice algebraic formulae is that they involve ",
  
  StyleBox["complex numbers",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and cannot be seen directly.  What we can do, however, is to map the \
inflection points to real points and then graph the transform of the curve.  \
Here is one transformation that could be used."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(tt = 
      FullSimplify[
        MQP[{p\_1, p\_2, r\_1, r\_2}, {w, u + w, 2\ v + w, 2\ u + v + w}]]; \n
    MatrixForm[tt]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That is not such a messy matrix!  Here is the transform of the polynomial \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["f",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g = Tidy[2\ \(TL[tt]\)[f]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here is the Hessian of the curve.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(k = Tidy[1\/864\ Det[MHP[g]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["We now graph them together.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{g == 0, k == 0} /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-4\), 6}, {y, \(-4\), 4}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100, 
      AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Black}, {Thickness[0.007], Red}}]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The three inflection points are apparent, and the three double \
points of the conic polars can be imagined.  For example, here is one of \
those points.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Tidy[FullSimplify[Tidy[1\/5\ \(TP[tt]\)[r\_3]\ 2]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Perhaps, there is a slight mystery as to what has happened to the \
double point of the original curve.  Here it is:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(d = Tidy[1\/16\ \(TP[tt]\)[w]\ 9\ \((2\/3 + \(2\ I\)\/\@3)\)]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["But it is real!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " Is this correct?  We need to check for this being a double point of the \
curve (and the Hessian)."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ExpandAll[{PD[g, d\^2], PD[k, d\^2]}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Well it satisfies the tests.  But it does not show up as a double point in \
the picture.  ",
  StyleBox["Why?",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The reason is that it is the only real point on a complex branch of the \
curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and so it is \"passing through\" the real plane at ",
  StyleBox["just one point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  The ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["ImplicitPlot",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "program does not plot single points, and so we cannot see it.  But it is \
there on the curve.  The same remarks apply to the Hessian."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox[
  "Another proof that all nodal cubics are projectively equivalent: ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "The plan of this proof is to make projective transformations until the \
general polynomial is in a ",
  StyleBox["canonical form",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Here is a general cubic to start with:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, cubic, Subscript]; \n
    cubic = Bxyz[3] . {a\_1, a\_2, a\_3, a\_4, a\_5, a\_6, a\_7, a\_8, a\_9, 
          a\_10}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We want ",
  StyleBox["w",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " to be a node for this curve, since wherever the node is it can be \
transformed to the origin.  The condition for the node is:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[cubic, w\^2] == 0\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This linear form can only vanish when the ",
  StyleBox["three coefficients ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "vanish.  Let us make this assignment and look again at the form of the \
cubic:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a\_8, a\_9, a\_10} = {0, 0, 0}; cubic\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "There are two tangents at the node.  We can make an aditional \
transformation to make the two tangents to be the lines ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["x",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["y",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  First we want to see what the tangents look like:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[cubic, w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For this to factor into a multiple of  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\ y\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " the outer coefficients must be zero."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({a\_5, a\_7} = {0, 0}; cubic\)], "Input"],

Cell["Note that the mixed terms have a common factor:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[%\[LeftDoubleBracket]2\[RightDoubleBracket] + %
          \[LeftDoubleBracket]3\[RightDoubleBracket] + %\[LeftDoubleBracket]5
          \[RightDoubleBracket]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can make a projective transformation that leaves ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["x",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["y",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " fixed but takes ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\ a\_2 + y\ a\_3 + z\ a\_6\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["z",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This would reduce the cubic to:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(cubic = %%\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket] + %%
          \[LeftDoubleBracket]4\[RightDoubleBracket] + x\ y\ z\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Using appropriate dilations along the ",
  StyleBox["x-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and ",
  StyleBox["y-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "axes, we can get rid of the two remaining coefficients:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(cubic = 
      cubic /. {x \[Rule] x\/a\_1\%\(1/3\), y \[Rule] y\/a\_4\%\(1/3\), 
          z \[Rule] a\_1\%\(1/3\)\ a\_4\%\(1/3\)\ z}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We have thus shown that ",
  StyleBox["every",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " nodal cubic is projectively equivalent to ",
  StyleBox["this specific curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Hence, they are all projectively equivalent.",
  StyleBox["  Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The nodal cubic as an Abelian group ", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Consider our ",
  StyleBox["standard nodal cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f, a, Subscript]; \nf = x\^3 + y\^3 - 6\ x\ y\ z; \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The following is its ",
  StyleBox["rational parameterization",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "(obtained by a method discussed previously):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(P[a_] := 6\ a\ u + 6\ a\^2\ v + \((1 + a\^3)\)\ w; \n
    Expand[PD[f, P[a]\^3]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We can find a nice condition in terms for the parameterization for three \
points on the curve to be ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[PE[P[a\_1], P[a\_2]], P[a\_3]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is zero for distinct points just in case ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_1\ a\_2\ a\_3 == \(-1\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Note the following point on the curve:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(Tidy[P[1]\/2]\), 
    \(PD[f, P[1]\^3]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We call this the ",
  StyleBox["unit point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Here is the condition for points to be collinear with it:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[PE[P[a\_1], P[a\_2]], P[1]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This algebra suggests looking at the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[I]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and and the tangent at that point."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(P[I]\), 
    \(t = Tidy[Last[Factor[PD[f, P[I]\^2]]]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It seems likely that the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on this tangent.  A similar conclusion would hold for the tangent at \
",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[\(-I\)]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[t, P[1]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Are there any other tangents at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "?  To answer this question, we have to find the envelope of the curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(e = Envel[f]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "It is of the ",
  StyleBox["4",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th degree, and the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[1]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is \"tangental\".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[Expand[PD[PD[f, P[1]\^2]\^3, e]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So the pencil at that point cuts the dual curve ",
  StyleBox["twice",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Hence, there are only ",
  StyleBox["three tangents",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " possible at the point (one of them being counted twice)."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
And here is the condition for six points on the curve to lie on a \
conic.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[PD[P2P[P[a\_1], P[a\_2], P[a\_3], P[a\_4], P[a\_5]], P[a\_6]\^2]]
      \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["That took a little time, but the result is ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["great!",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" Namely,  ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["a",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], " ", 
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["a",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], " ", 
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["a",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"], " ", 
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["a",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "4"], " ", 
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["a",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "5"], " ", 
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["a",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "6"]}], " ", "==", " ", "1"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[" is the condition for distinct points ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"P", "[", 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["a",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"P", "[", 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["a",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"P", "[", 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["a",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"P", "[", 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["a",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "4"], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"P", "[", 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["a",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "5"], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"P", "[", 
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["a",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "6"], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]],
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["on the cubic",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["to lie on a ",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  StyleBox["conic",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Excluding the one parameter value ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "(which corresponds to the node ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "), all the other points on the nodal cubic form a group isomorphic to the \
",
  StyleBox["multiplicative group",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the non-zero complex numbers.  "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "The group operation can be defined as follows:  Take ",
  StyleBox["two points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the curve.  Join them by a ",
  StyleBox["line",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Find the ",
  StyleBox["third intersection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of this line with the cubic.  Join this point with the ",
  StyleBox["unit point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Find the ",
  StyleBox["third intersection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of this second line with the curve.  That point is the answer, called the \
",
  StyleBox["product",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the two points.  (This is exactly the ",
  StyleBox["same procedure",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " as we carried out for the cuspidal cubic \[LongDash] except we are using \
a ",
  StyleBox["different",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " curve with a ",
  StyleBox["different",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " shape and, so, get a ",
  StyleBox["different",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " structure.)  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The proof that the operation just defined corresponds to ",
  StyleBox["multiplication",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["via",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the parameterization uses the results above characterizing the collinear \
points on the curve.  In particular, we note that the two triples of points\n\
\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({P[a], P[b], P[\(-\(1\/a . b\)\)]}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({P[a\ b], P[1], P[\(-\(1\/a . b\)\)]}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\nare always ",
  StyleBox["collinear triples",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  That shows how to get from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[b]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to their \"product\" according to the geometric construction.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "By the way, the line joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(P[a]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the curve to the origin ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "has slope ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["a",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\(ClearAll[a]; \)\), 
    \(Factor[PE[w, P[a]]]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We of course know that the multiplicative group of complex numbers has ",
  StyleBox["non-trivial cube roots",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ",  because the equation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\^3 == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has non-trivial solutions.",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " (The technical term is that the group has ",
  StyleBox["torsion",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".)  On the other hand, the ",
  StyleBox["additive group",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " has ",
  StyleBox["no",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " non-trivial solutions to the equation ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(3\ z == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Therefore, the group structures of the ",
  StyleBox["nodal cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " and the ",
  StyleBox["cuspidal cubic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " are definitely ",
  StyleBox["non isomorphic",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (And so this is another reason those two curves are ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " projectively equivalent.)  As we discussed earlier, the solutions to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(z\^3 == \(-1\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " correspond to the ",
  StyleBox["three inflection points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since the cubic has been made into a group, we can look at some of its ",
  StyleBox["subgroups",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Here is a portion of one."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(grp = Table[P[2\^\(i/3\)], {i, \(-15\), 15}]; \n\(pict = Draw[grp]; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
The points are arranged arount the top bow of the curve.  If we \
continued the exponents to hogher powers, the points would converge to the \
double point but not reach it.  From the recipe for making the group \
operation, we can derive some interesting configurations.  For example, let \
us join some of the points in the subgroup together in a systemetic way and \
then graph the results.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(lines = {
        N[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]3\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]15\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0, 
        N[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]4\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]14\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0, 
        N[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]5\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]13\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0, 
        N[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]6\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]12\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0, 
        N[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]7\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]11\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0, 
        N[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]8\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]10\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0, \n\t\t
        N[PD[f, grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]9\[RightDoubleBracket]\^2]] == 0, \n
        \t\tN[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]7\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]18\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0, 
        N[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]8\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]17\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0, 
        N[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]9\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]16\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0, 
        N[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]10\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]15\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0, 
        N[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]11\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]14\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0, 
        N[PE[grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]12\[RightDoubleBracket], 
              grp\[LeftDoubleBracket]13\[RightDoubleBracket]]] == 0}; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[Join[lines, {f == 0}] /. z \[Rule] 1, \n
      \t{x, \(-4\), 4}, {y, \(-4\), 4}, AxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 70, 
      PlotStyle 
        \[Rule] {{Thickness[0.007], Blue}, {Thickness[0.007], Blue}, {
            Thickness[0.007], Blue}, {Thickness[0.007], Blue}, {
            Thickness[0.007], Blue}, {Thickness[0.007], Blue}, {
            Thickness[0.007], Blue}, {Thickness[0.007], Green}, {
            Thickness[0.007], Green}, {Thickness[0.007], Green}, {
            Thickness[0.007], Green}, {Thickness[0.007], Green}, {
            Thickness[0.007], Green}, {Thickness[0.008], Black}}]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We get two pencils of lines with their centers lying on the curve.  Note \
that we could keep drawing this grid using the lines already drawn in to \
start and by finding intersections with the curve.  This suggests that there \
is a crossratio result involved, and indeed any four consecutive points in \
any such subgroup make a ",
  StyleBox["constant crossratio",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(RL[PE[w, P[a\^n]], PE[w, P[a\^\(n + 1\)]], PE[w, P[a\^\(n + 2\)]], 
      PE[w, P[a\^\(n + 3\)]]]\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Classifying Non-singular Cubics", "Section"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Normal forms for non-singular cubics", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The three normal forms", "Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
There are three theorems about normal forms producing simple but \
rather different- looking equations.  We will need to examine the \
relationships between them.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Every non-singular cubic is ",
  StyleBox["projectively equivalent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to one defined by an equation of the form:\n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(y\^2\ z == x\ \((x - z)\)\ \((x - e\ z)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",\t\nwhere ",
  StyleBox["e",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["neither",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["nor",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["1",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\t"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Every non-singular cubic is ",
  StyleBox["projectively equivalent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to one defined by an equation of the form:\n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(y\^2\ z == 4\ x\^3 - x\ z\^2\ g\_2 - z\^3\ g\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",\nwhere ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g\_2\%3 - 27\ g\_3\%2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\t"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Note:",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The choice of notation here is related to the use of the Weierstra\[SZ] \
",
  StyleBox["\[WeierstrassP]",
    FontFamily->"Symbol",
    FontWeight->"Plain"],
  " function and the parameterization of cubics.  This function is described \
in lecture notebook 15."
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Plain",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Every non-singular cubic is ",
  StyleBox["projectively equivalent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to one defined by an equation of the form:\n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\^3 + y\^3 + z\^3 == 3 k\ x\ y\ z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ",\nwhere ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(k\^3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["1",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The first normal form", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof of the theorem:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  To understand the condition imposed on ",
  StyleBox["e",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", we rewrite the normal-form polynomial as follows in order to see the \
coefficients:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(ClearAll[f, g, h, Subscript]\), 
    \(f = Tidy[y\^2\ z - x\ \((x - z)\)\ \((x - e\ z)\)]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Of course, the original form was chosen so we could see that the curve \
must intersect the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["x",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "-axis where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x == e\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We are making sure these three intersections are ",
  StyleBox["distinct ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "by the condition on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Note that, in the expanded form, the pair of coefficients",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{"{", 
        StyleBox[\(\((1 + e)\), \(-e\)\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["}",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " sum to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["1",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and the pairs ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({2, \(-1\)}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({1, 0}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are the ones excluded."
}], "Text"],

Cell["Here is the Hessian curve: ", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h = Tidy[1\/8\ Det[MHP[f]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Unfortunately",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it is hard to see off hand whether this curve is singular or not."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Returning to the general curve, we recall that a non-singular cubic has ",
  StyleBox["no",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " double points and (we hope) ",
  StyleBox["nine",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (distinct) ",
  StyleBox["inflection points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Consider such a curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f\_0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Strictly speaking, at the outset of the discussion all we need is the \
existence of ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " inflection \[LongDash] and we have that, because the non-zero Hessian ",
  StyleBox["must",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " intersect the curve at least once.  When we get the right normal form and \
see that the Hessian has ",
  StyleBox["no double points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then we will ",
  StyleBox["know",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " there have to be ",
  StyleBox["nine",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " inflections."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let one inflection point of the curve ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f\_0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We know that the conic polar ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["f",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "0"], 
            StyleBox[",",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["p",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "]"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "at that point degenerates into",
  StyleBox[" two distinct lines",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", one of which is the ",
  StyleBox["tangent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to the curve.  Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be the ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the conic polar (which means that the derivative ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f\_0, p\ q] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ").  The point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " will ",
  StyleBox["not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " be on the cubic, since the only point on the curve also on the tangent \
has to be",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " itself.  (Why?)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now the conic polar hits the curve at ",
  StyleBox["the points of tangency",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the tangents drawn from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", so (aside from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ") there should be  ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of these, all ",
  StyleBox["collinear",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " with with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)  If two of these points were equal (say, at a point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the intersection of the conic polar and the cubic), then the line \
joining ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " would be tangent to the curve.  This would mean we would have\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(PD[f\_0, p\ r\^2] == PD[f\_0, q\ r\^2] == PD[f\_0, r\ r\^2] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nBut then, because the three points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["p",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["q",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are ",
  StyleBox["independent",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ",",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["f",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "0"], ",", \(r\^2\)}], "]"}], "==", 
        "0"}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " would follow, which is ",
  StyleBox["impossible",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " because the curve ",
  StyleBox["does not",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " have a ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["r",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " be one of the intersection points (and keep in mind there are two others, \
with all three distinct).  By a suitable projective transformation, we can \
move ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({p, \ q, \ r}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, \ v, \ w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  The other two intersections of the conic polar to the inflection point \
are then along the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  One of the other points can be made into ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by an additional projective transformation leaving ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " fixed ",
  StyleBox["as points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " (not as vectors, of course).   Finally the third intersection can be \
notated as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(e\ u + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", where ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(e \[NotEqual] 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(e \[NotEqual] 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  (Why?)  Let us call the ",
  StyleBox["transformed curve",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Comment.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "We remark that the crossratio of the points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(e\ u + w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is simply ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " itself.  This number (or more precisely the ",
  StyleBox["six numbers generated",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " from it by ",
  StyleBox["permuting",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the four points and taking crossratios) will be shown to be the ",
  StyleBox["projective invariant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the curve among non-singular cubics.  The reason is that this ratio is \
determined by the tangent at the inflection point and the three other \
tangents from that point.  The key observation here is that the ",
  StyleBox["same set of ratios",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " is determined at ",
  StyleBox["each",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the nine inflection points.  That is how we get a projective \
invariant.  (Why?)"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "These considerations about the ",
  StyleBox["convenient placing of points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " give us in the first place six equations about thes points ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({u, v, w}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " as related to the transformed curve:\n\t",
  StyleBox["Equation\t\t\t   Meaning",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\n(1)\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f\_1, v\^3] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\t\t    \"",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the cubic\"\n(2)\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f\_1, w\^3] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\t\t    \"",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the cubic\"\n(3)\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            StyleBox[\(f\_1, u\ v\^2\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "]"}], "==", "0"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "      \t    \"",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the tangent at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\"\n(4)\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f\_1, u\^2\ v] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\t    \"",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the conic polar of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\"\n(5)\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(PD[f\_1, u\ v\ w] == 0\)],
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "\t    \"",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " becomes double point of conic polar\"\n(6)\t ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"PD", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              SubscriptBox[
                StyleBox["f",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], "1"], 
              StyleBox[",",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[\(v\ w\^2\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "]"}], "==", "0"}],
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\t    \"",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is on the tangent at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\""
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The import of equations (3)\[Dash](5) is to make ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f\_1, u\ v] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)  And, these conditions are enough to make ",
  StyleBox["all points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\ u + b\ v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " on the line ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["z",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lie on the conic polar ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(PD[f\_1, v]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " starts out as a cubic polynomial, which in general can be given the \
following general form."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f\_1 = {a\_1, 3\ a\_2, 3\ a\_3, a\_4, 3\ a\_5, 6\ a\_6, 3\ a\_7, 
          3\ a\_8, 3\ a\_9, a\_10} . Bxyz[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But this polynomial has to ",
  StyleBox["annihilate",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " these six monomials as remarked above."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[f\_1, #]&)\)/@{u\^2\ v, u\ v\^2, v\^3, u\ v\ w, v\ w\^2, w\^3}
        \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Of course!  It means that the corresponding ",
  StyleBox["coefficients",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " vanish.  So, this is what ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "has to be:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f\_1 = {a\_1, 0, 0, 0, 3\ a\_5, 0, 3\ a\_7, 3\ a\_8, 0, 0} . 
        Bxyz[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " cannot be zero, since otherwise the polynomial would ",
  StyleBox["factor",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  So, by a projective transformation, we can assume ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_7 == \(-\(a\_1\/3\)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", because that is the only term involving ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["y",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)  We can thus transform the polynomial to:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f\_2 = {a\_1, 0, 0, 0, 3\ a\_5, 0, \(-a\_1\), 3\ a\_8, 0, 0} . 
        Bxyz[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now we can divide through by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(-a\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["rename",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the other coefficients:  Thus, every non-singular cubic can be \
transformed into equivalent to this:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, e]; \n
    f\_3 = {\(-1\), 0, 0, 0, a, 0, 1, b, 0, 0} . Bxyz[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "However, we must remember that the original cubic was transformed to pass \
through two ",
  StyleBox["other points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " points on the ",
  StyleBox["x-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "axis.  Let us find the values of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["a",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " that make this true."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = 
      Solve[{PD[f\_3, \((u + w)\)\^3] == 0, 
          PD[f\_3, \((e\ u + w)\)\^3] == 0}, {a, b}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["We then rewrite the polynomial for the last time.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f\_3 = f\_3 /. sol\[LeftDoubleBracket]1\[RightDoubleBracket]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And this is the result we wanted.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["Here is a way to generate pictures.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[0 == f\_3 /. {z \[Rule] 1, e \[Rule] \(-2\)}, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tAxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 50, PlotStyle \[Rule] {Thickness[0.006]}]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[0 == f\_3 /. {z \[Rule] 1, e \[Rule] 3}, \n
      \t{x, \(-3\), 4}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, \n\tAxesOrigin \[Rule] {0, 0}, 
      PlotPoints \[Rule] 50, PlotStyle \[Rule] {Thickness[0.006]}]; \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["And note the following crossratio.  ", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(RP[u, w, u + w, 3  u + w]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "In the course of the proof, we came across an ",
  StyleBox["intermediate form",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  So, we ask:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Is a cubic of the form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(-x\^3\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["+",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{
            StyleBox["\[Alpha]",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], " ", 
            StyleBox[\(x\^2\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[" ",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["z",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["+",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(y\^2\ z\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["+",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(\[Beta]\ x\ z\^2\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["ever",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " singular?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Answer:",
  StyleBox[" If ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  "both",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(\[Alpha] == \[Beta] == 0\)],
    FontSlant->"Plain"],
  StyleBox[
  ", we already know the cubic is singular.  We can attack the more general \
question of the double point directly.",
    FontWeight->"Plain",
    FontSlant->"Plain"]
}], "Text",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{\(ClearAll[g]\), ";", "\n", 
      RowBox[{"g", "=", 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox[\(-x\^3\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["+",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["\[Alpha]",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], " ", 
              StyleBox[\(x\^2\),
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox[" ",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["z",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}],
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["+",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[\(y\^2\ z\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["+",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox[\(\[Beta]\ x\ z\^2\),
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}], 
      StyleBox[";",
        FontWeight->"Bold"], "\n", 
      \(trial = PD[g, \((a\ u\  + \ b\ v\  + c\ w)\)\^2] // Tidy\)}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Hmmmm.  For that to be the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " form, ",
  StyleBox["either",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " ",
  StyleBox["or",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["c",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has to be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  If they ",
  StyleBox["both",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["vanish",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the point we are trying make a double point is just equivalent to ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  And:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(PD[g, u\^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see it is ",
  StyleBox["never",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " a double point.   "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Case 1.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Suppose ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(b == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We can assume ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)  So, we try to see when the remaining coefficients could \
possibly be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Eliminate[{\((2\ b\^2 + 2\ a\^2\ \[Alpha] + 4\ a\ c\ \[Beta])\) == 0, \ 
        \((\(-6\)\ a\^2 + 4\ a\ c\ \[Alpha] + 2\ c\^2\ \[Beta])\) == 0, 
        b == 0, c == 1}, {a, b, c}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Assuming we can ",
  StyleBox["trust",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it looks as though ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\[Beta] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\[Beta] == \(-\(\[Alpha]\^2\/4\)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are the only posibilities.  In the first case we have an obvious double \
point:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(g /. \[Beta] \[Rule] 0\n\t\t\t\t\), 
    \(PD[%, w\^2]\)}], "Input"],

Cell["In the second case, we have to do more work.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(g /. {\[Beta] \[Rule] \(-\(\[Alpha]\^2\/4\)\)}\n\t\t\t\t\), 
    \(PD[%, \((a\ u\  + w)\)\^2] // Tidy\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["\[Alpha]",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is not ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (Why?), neither can ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", if this is to be the ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " form.  (Why?)  From the vanishing of the second coefficient we see ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a == \[Alpha]\/2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Does this kill the first coefficient?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-6\)\ a\^2 + 4\ a\ \[Alpha] - \[Alpha]\^2\/2 /. {
        a \[Rule] \[Alpha]\/2}\)], "Input"],

Cell["Yes.  Whew!!  ", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Case 2.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Suppose ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We can assume ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(b == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  (Why?)  So, we try to see when the remaining coefficients could \
possibly be ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Eliminate[{\((2\ b\^2 + 2\ a\^2\ \[Alpha] + 4\ a\ c\ \[Beta])\) == 0, \ 
        \((\(-6\)\ a\^2 + 4\ a\ c\ \[Alpha] + 2\ c\^2\ \[Beta])\) == 0, 
        c == 0, b == 1}, {a, b, c}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "No solutions!  Do we believe it?  Hmmm.  Yes, it is obvious.  (Why?) \
Whew!!!!  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["So we have proved:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  A",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " cubic of the form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(-x\^3\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["+",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{
            StyleBox["\[Alpha]",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], " ", 
            StyleBox[\(x\^2\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox[" ",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["z",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["+",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(y\^2\ z\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["+",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[\(\[Beta]\ x\ z\^2\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["singular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if, and only if,  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\[Beta] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{\(\[Alpha]\^2\), "+", 
            RowBox[{"4", 
              StyleBox["\[Beta]",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["0",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And note that in each of the two cases we identified the ",
  StyleBox["double point",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The second normal form", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof of the theorem:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "The second version of the normal form uses a projective transformation \
from a previous type of cubic of the ",
  StyleBox["crossing points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " on the ",
  StyleBox["x-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "axis by means of a carefully chosen translation together with a change of \
scale on the ",
  StyleBox["y",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " axis.  First we give the general form of the matrix of such a \
transformation:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, d, e, f, g, M, Subscript]; \n
    M = MQP[{w, u + w, v + w, v + u + w}, \n
        \t\t{a\ u + b\ w, \((1 + a)\)\ u + b\ w, c\ v + a\ u + b\ w, 
          c\ v + \((1 + a)\)\ u + b\ w}]; \nM // MatrixForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Then, by \"intuiting\" the right substitutions for ",
  StyleBox["a",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and ",
  StyleBox["c",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " and by multiplying ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "by a scalar, we simplify ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(f\_1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Clearly, the parameter ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is not ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["M",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is to be ",
  StyleBox["non singular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f\_1 = \(-x\^3\) + \((1 + e)\)\ x\^2\ z + y\^2\ z - e\ x\ z\^2; \n
    f\_2 = Map[Factor, 
        Tidy[\(TL[M\/2\^\(2/3\)]\)[f\_1] /. {
              a \[Rule] \(-\(1\/3\)\)\ \((1 + e)\), c \[Rule] 2\/\@b}], {2}]
        \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, we can simplify the first coefficient involving ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by solving this equation for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[4\ \((1 - e + e\^2)\) == 3\ \(b\^2\) g\_2, {e}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Let us try one of these substitutions and some algebra:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f\_2 = 
      Map[Factor, 
        Tidy[Together[
            f\_2 /. e \[Rule] 
                1\/2\ \((1 + \@3\ \@\(\(-1\) + b\^2\ g\_2\))\)]], {2}]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Amazing! ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  It is the ",
  StyleBox["same",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " polynomial with a use of ",
  StyleBox["different names",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for part of the coefficients: we got rid of any mention of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and confined ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to the one coefficient.  The question is whether that last coefficient \
can become ",
  StyleBox["any other number",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "with a suitable choice of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "?   ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "'s algebra, unfortunately, is rather too much of a ",
  StyleBox["black box",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to be able to see exactly how the conclusion is reached.  For instance, \
if we ask whether ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " can be ",
  StyleBox["eliminated",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " from the following equation, we find:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Eliminate[{
        \(\((\(-4\) + b\^2\ g\_2)\)\ \@\(\(-1\) + b\^2\ g\_2\)\)\/\(3\ \@3\ 
              b\^3\) == g\_3}, {b}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "we get a",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.700008, 0]],
  StyleBox["go-ahead",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.700008, 0]],
  StyleBox[".",
    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.700008, 0]],
  "  ",
  StyleBox["But",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", when we try to ",
  StyleBox["see",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the solution, we get ",
  StyleBox["very long",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " expressions:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[
      \((\(-4\) + b\^2\ g\_2)\)\ 
          \@\(\(-1\) + b\^2\ g\_2\)/\((3\ \@3\ b\^3)\) == g\_3, {b}]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "These six solutions are hard to interpret.  In particular, it looks as \
though the quantity ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g\_2\%3 - 27\ g\_3\%2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " has to be ",
  StyleBox["non zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We have to find a way to check up on this in another context.  "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So we conclude \[LongDash] ",
  StyleBox["somewhat by faith, alas",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " \[LongDash] that by a suitable choice of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " the normal form can be made into the following polynomial:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(f\_3 = {\(-4\), 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, g\_2, 0, g\_3} . Bxyz[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "As an ",
  StyleBox["equation",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " for a cubic, we usually write:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(y\^2\ z == y\^2\ z - f\_3\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Intermediate Question.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Could such a cubic be singular?    "
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We had best make a direct attack again."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(check = Tidy[PD[f\_3, \((a\ u + b\ v + c\ w)\)\^2]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Case 1.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We try ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(b == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(check /. {b \[Rule] 0, c \[Rule] 1}\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Eliminate[{\ \((\(-24\)\ a\^2 + 2\ g\_2)\) == 0, \ 
        \((4\ a\ g\_2 + 6\ g\_3)\) == 0}, {a}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Ah, that looks familiar!", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Case 2.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "We try ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(b == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(c == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(check /. {b \[Rule] 1, c \[Rule] 0}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Never vanishes!  The investigation is complete.  Singularity happens only \
when ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g\_2\%3 - 27\ g\_3\%2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is",
  StyleBox[" ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["Here is something else we should ask.", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Can we get any better information about the Hessian?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  All we can do is to try.  Here is the Hessian:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h\_3 = Tidy[\(-\(1\/8\)\)\ Det[MHP[f\_3]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here is the dual curve (= envelope):", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(d\_3 = Envel[f\_3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Case 1.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g\_2 == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then the Hessian factors into three non-concurrent lines."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-12\)\ x\ \((y - \@3\ z\ \@g\_3)\)\ \((y + \@3\ z\ \@g\_3)\) // Expand
      \)], "Input"],

Cell["Here are the intersections.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(PE[\ x, \ \((y - \@3\ z\ \@g\_3)\)]\n\), 
    \(PE[\ x, \ \((y + \@3\ z\ \@g\_3)\)]\n\), 
    \(PE[\ y - \@3\ z\ \@g\_3, \ \((y + \@3\ z\ \@g\_3)\)]\)}], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "But ",
  StyleBox["none",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the lines is a tangent!  (Why?  Look:)"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((PD[#\^6, d\_3]&)\)/@{\(-12\)\ x, y - \@3\ z\ \@g\_3, 
          y + \@3\ z\ \@g\_3} /. g\_2 \[Rule] 0\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "And ",
  StyleBox["none",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the intersections is on the curve."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(PD[f\_3 /. g\_2 \[Rule] 0, #\^3]&\)\ /@
      \ {w + \@3\ v\ \@g\_3, w - \@3\ v\ \@g\_3, \n2\ \@3\ u\ \@g\_3}\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Ha, ha!  The curve ",
  StyleBox["must",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " have ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["9",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " inflection points! "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Case 2.",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["non zero",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", then we projectively transform the Hessian."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Tidy[
      \(-\(h\_3\/g\_2\%2\)\) /. {x \[Rule] g\_2\ z, z \[Rule] x, 
          y \[Rule] \(\@g\_2\ y\)\/\@12}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Ho,ho!  Now, we proved earlier that a cubic of the form ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(\(-x\^3\) + y\^2\ z + x\^2\ z\ \[Alpha] + x\ z\^2\ \[Beta]\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  StyleBox["singular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if, and only if,  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\[Beta] == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{\(\[Alpha]\^2\), "+", 
            RowBox[{"4", 
              StyleBox["\[Beta]",
                FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["0",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ".  Since ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is non zero, we need only check the vanishing of:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(\[Alpha]\^2 + 4 \[Beta]\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["/.",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["{",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          RowBox[{
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["\[Alpha]",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              StyleBox["\[Rule]",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], 
              RowBox[{
                StyleBox["-",
                  FontWeight->"Bold"], \(\(36\ \ g\_3\)\/g\_2\)}]}], 
            StyleBox[",",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], \(\[Beta] \[Rule] \(-12\)\ g\_2\)}], 
          "}"}]}], "//", "Factor"}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Rah! Rah! Rah!  We already know why it is nonzero, so the Hessian is non \
singular!  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Therefore",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", by investigating normal forms we have proved a key conclusion:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  T",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "he non-singular cubic always has ",
  StyleBox["nine inflections",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["One other thought:", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Question.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  Just to be sure, should we not ask whether we can  ",
  StyleBox["get back ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "from the second normal form to the first?"
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 0]],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Answer:",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  To follow the backward path, we need to compare the polynomial:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\(-4\)\ x\^3 + y\^2\ z + x\ z\^2\ g\_2 + z\^3\ g\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "\nto the polynomial we found by transformation above:\n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(\(-4\)\ x\^3 + y\^2\ z + 
          \(4\ \((1 - e + e\^2)\)\ x\ z\^2\)\/\(3\ b\^2\) + 
          \(4\ \((\(-2\) + e)\)\ \((1 + e)\)\ \((\(-1\) + 2\ e)\)\ 
              z\^3\)\/\(27\ b\^3\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]]
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We find that we need is to have:\n",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(4\ \((1 - e + e\^2)\) == b\^2\ 3\ g\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(4\ \((\(-2\) + e)\)\ \((1 + e)\)\ \((\(-1\) + 2\ e)\) == \ 
          \(b\^3\) 27\ g\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nLet us eliminate ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["b",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " from this equation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{\(ClearAll[e]\), ";", "\n", 
      RowBox[{"equ", "=", "\n", 
        RowBox[{"Factor", "/@", 
          RowBox[{"Eliminate", "[", 
            RowBox[{
              RowBox[{"{", 
                RowBox[{
                  StyleBox[\(4\ \((1 - e + e\^2)\) == b\^2\ 3\ g\_2\),
                    FontWeight->"Bold"], " ", ",", " ", 
                  
                  StyleBox[
                    \(4\ \((\(-2\) + e)\)\ \((1 + e)\)\ 
                        \((\(-1\) + 2\ e)\) == \ \(b\^3\) 27\ g\_3\),
                    FontWeight->"Bold"]}], "}"}], ",", \({b}\)}], 
            "]"}]}]}]}]], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "That is not so bad.  This ",
  StyleBox["6",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "th-degree equation allows us to solve for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in terms of the coefficients ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g\_3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  We can try to find out how such solutions should look with a slightly \
simpler notation."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ans = 
      Solve[\((\(-2\) + e)\)\^2\ \((1 + e)\)\^2\ \((\(-1\) + 2\ e)\)\^2\ r == 
          108\ \((1 - e + e\^2)\)\^3\ s, {e}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Ugh!  We look at the solution without ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["I",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " in hopes the algebra is better."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(e\_1 = Together[e /. ans\[LeftDoubleBracket]5\[RightDoubleBracket]]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["\<\
By looking at the various parts of this expression, we find many \
can factor.  So, we can make a replacement (possibly by running this command \
twice).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(e\_1 = 
      e\_1 /. {\(-r\^5\)\ s + 81\ r\^4\ s\^2 - 2187\ r\^3\ s\^3 + 
              19683\ r\^2\ s\^4 \[Rule] \(-r\^2\)\ \((r - 27\ s)\)\^3\ s, 
          r\^4 - 567\ r\^3\ s + 8748\ r\^2\ s\^2 + 157464\ r\ s\^3 \[Rule] 
            r\ \((r - 27\ s)\)\ \((r\^2 - 540\ r\ s - 5832\ s\^2)\), 
          r\^2 - 54\ r\ s + 729\ s\^2 \[Rule] \((r - 27\ s)\)\^2, 
          5\ r\^2 - 27\ r\ s - 2916\ s\^2 \[Rule] 
            \((r - 27\ s)\)\ \((5\ r + 108\ s)\), 
          r\^3 + 189\ r\^2\ s - 5832\ r\ s\^2 \[Rule] 
            r\ \((r - 27\ s)\)\ \((r + 216\ s)\), 
          r\^3 - 540\ r\^2\ s - 5832\ r\ s\^2 \[Rule] 
            r\ \((r\^2 - 540\ r\ s - 5832\ s\^2)\)}\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Still not too good.  We can introduce an abbreviation to shorten \
things.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(e\_1 = e\_1 /. {r\^2 - 540\ r\ s - 5832\ s\^2 \[Rule] t}\)], "Input"],

Cell["What is under the one radical factors.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((\(-1152\)\ \@\(\(-r\^2\)\ \((r - 27\ s)\)\^3\ s\)\ 
            \((\@3\ r - 27\ \@3\ s)\) - 48\ r\ \((r - 27\ s)\)\ t - 
          48\ r\ \((r - 27\ s)\)\ \((r + 216\ s)\)\ 
            \((24\ \@3\ \@\(\(-r\^2\)\ \((r - 27\ s)\)\^3\ s\) + r\ t)\)\^\(1/
                3\) + 16\ \((r - 27\ s)\)\^2\ 
            \((24\ \@3\ \@\(\(-r\^2\)\ \((r - 27\ s)\)\^3\ s\) + r\ t)\)\^\(2/
                3\) + 16\ \((r - 27\ s)\)\ \((5\ r + 108\ s)\)\ 
            \((24\ \@3\ \@\(\(-r\^2\)\ \((r - 27\ s)\)\^3\ s\) + r\ t)\)\^\(2/
                3\))\) // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Hmmmmm.  Maybe this could lead to some simplifications.  But it does not \
seem worthwhile to continue.   We conclude it is ",
  StyleBox["possible",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " to go back to the other equation and solve for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.F.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["We go back to the original equation.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(epoly = Expand[First[equ] - Last[equ]]\)], "Input"],

Cell["Here is another view of the coefficients.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(CoefficientList[epoly, {e}] // TableForm\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that the equation for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " should not have ",
  StyleBox["0",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", ",
  StyleBox["ComplexInfinity",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ", or ",
  StyleBox["1",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " as a root.  This means that neither the leading, last, or sum of the \
coefficients of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["epoly",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " should be zero.  These come down to simple expressions:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(epoly /. e \[Rule] 0 // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(epoly /. e \[Rule] 1 // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(Last[Together[epoly\/e\^6]] /. e -> 0 // Expand\) // Factor\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "The quantity ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g\_2\%3 - 27\ g\_3\%2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is called the ",
  StyleBox["discriminant",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the cubic polynomial: ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox[\(4\ x\^3\),
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["-",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          RowBox[{
            SubscriptBox[
              StyleBox["g",
                FontWeight->"Bold"], "2"], 
            StyleBox[" ",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["x",
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}],
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["-",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          SubscriptBox[
            StyleBox["g",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ".  The non vanishing means that this polynomial has ",
  StyleBox["three distinct roots",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", that is to say, the cubic curve cuts the ",
  StyleBox["x-",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "axis in three distinct points \[LongDash] which is of course what we \
needed to have a non-singular curve as we already argued."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that the determination of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is not ",
  StyleBox["unique",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", as ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["epoly",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a polynomial of degree ",
  StyleBox["6",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  ".  Because the polynomial is ",
  StyleBox["symmetric in its coefficients",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", it is clear that if ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is a root, then so is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(1\/e\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Let us see the effect of the transformations ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(e \[Rule] 1 - e\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  StyleBox["  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(e \[Rule] \(e - 1\)\/e\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "on the roots of the polynomial:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(epoly\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(epoly == Expand[\(e\^6\) \((epoly /. e \[Rule] 1\/e)\)]\ \)\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(epoly == Expand[epoly /. e \[Rule] 1 - e]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(epoly == Expand[\(e\^6\) \((epoly /. e \[Rule] \(e - 1\)\/e)\)\ ]\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This proves that the roots of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(epoly\  == \ 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " are the set of ",
  StyleBox["six numbers ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "associated with a crossratio of four points (in all orders).  To find an \
expression for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " we can solve for the intersections with the ",
  StyleBox["x",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "-axis.  The algebra does not seem too wonderful."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Finally, there is an invariant associated with ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " called the ",
  StyleBox["modulus",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the curve.  We note the the equation for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["e",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " can also be written as: "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "\t \t      ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(\(4\ \((1 - e + e\^2)\)\^3\)\/\(\((e + 1)\)\^2\ \((2\ e - 1)\)\^2\ 
              \((e - 2)\)\^2\) == g\_2\%3\/\(27\ g\_3\%2\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nSo this means all the six roots give this quotient on the left a \
constant value (",
  StyleBox["different",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " from ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["1",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ").  (Why?)  And curves with a different set of crossratios give this \
quantity a different value.  So we conclude:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "Two non-singular cubics are projectively equivalent if, and only if, they \
have the ",
  StyleBox["same modulus",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "."
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell["By the way, the modulus can be written in a different way.", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(4\ \((1 - e + e\^2)\)\^3\)\/\(\((e + 1)\)\^2\ \((2\ e - 1)\)\^2\ 
          \((e - 2)\)\^2\) // Apart\)], "Input"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The third normal form", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Proof of the theorem:  ",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "The third version of the normal form is proved by using several \
\[LongDash] not very obvious \[LongDash] projective transformations from an \
intermediate normal form which we get by positioning the inflection points.  \
We start with a general (non-singular) cubic."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[g, h, k, a, b, Subscript]; 
    g = {a\_1, a\_2, a\_3, a\_4, a\_5, a\_6, a\_7, a\_8, a\_9, a\_10} . 
        Bxyz[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "From previous work, we know there must be at least ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " collinear inflection points.  The line joining them and the three \
tangents at the inflection points will form a non-degenerate ",
  StyleBox["quadrilateral",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  We are going to imagine moving that figure so that the inflection \
points are at infinity and the sides of the resulting quadrilateral are the \
lines ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["x",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["y",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["z",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x + y + z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  This will mean that the inflection points are ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u - v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Moreover, ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["w",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is the intersection of the tangents at ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["u",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["v",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".  Thinking that we had already moved ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["g",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " so this would be so, the following four quantities must vanish:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({PD[g, u\^3], PD[g, v\^3], PD[g, \(u\^2\) w], PD[g, \(v\^2\) w]}\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell["Let us record this simplification:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g\_1 = 
      g /. {\ a\_1 \[Rule] 0, a\_4 \[Rule] 0, a\_5 \[Rule] 0, a\_7 \[Rule] 0}
          \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Since ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(u - v\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " must be on the curve, and since the point ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(v - w\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " lies on the tangent ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x + y + z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", the following two quantities must also vanish:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({PD[g\_1, \((u - v)\)\^3], PD[g\_1, \(\((u - v)\)\^2\) \((v - w)\)]}
      \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see this implies that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_2 == a\_3 == a\_6\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[{\(-6\)\ a\_2 + 6\ a\_3 == 0, 2\ a\_2 - 4\ a\_3 + 2\ a\_6 == 0}, {
        a\_3, a\_6}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["So we can make a further simplification:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g\_2 = g\_1 /. {a\_3 \[Rule] a\_2, a\_6 \[Rule] a\_2}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " cannot vanish, since otherwise the curve would be degenerate.  The form \
is not simple enough yet \[LongDash] because we have not really said that we \
have ",
  StyleBox["inflection",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " ",
  StyleBox["points",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "!  To have inflections where we want them, the following three quantities \
must vanish:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({Det[MHP[PD[g\_2, u]]], Det[MHP[PD[g\_2, v]]], 
      Factor[Det[MHP[PD[g\_2, u - v]]]]}\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Hmmmm.  If two are ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", then so is the third.  Why?  Ah, yes, the line joining two inflection \
points must cut the cubic at a third inflection point.  In any case, we see \
both ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_8\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " and ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_9\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " have to go away.  We make another simplification:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g\_3 = g\_2 /. {a\_8 \[Rule] 0, a\_9 \[Rule] 0}\)], "Input"],

Cell["That is better!  Part of the polynomial factors:", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\^2\ y\ a\_2 + x\ y\^2\ a\_2 + x\ y\ z\ a\_2 // Factor\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "So, if we divide by ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", and call ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(a\_10/a\_2\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " by another name, we see our original polynomial can be written in the \
form:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(g\_4 = d\ z\^3 + x\ y\ \((x + y + z)\); \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
That is a good normal form in itself!  Here is what such curves \
look like:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(ImplicitPlot[{g\_4 == 0} /. {d \[Rule] \(-\(1\/2\)\), z \[Rule] 1}, {
        x, \(-3\), 3}, {y, \(-3\), 3}, PlotPoints \[Rule] 100]; \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
And we can see the three inflection points off at infinity (well, \
almost).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Now a linear substitution can be arranged to balance the three \
variables better:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g\_5 = 
      Tidy[27\ g\_4 /. {x \[Rule] 1\/3\ \((\(-x\) + 2\ y - z)\), 
            y \[Rule] 1\/3\ \((2\ x - y - z)\), z \[Rule] z}]\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Of course, getting this new form under a projective transformation, \
will move the inflection points around.  But never mind that.  The point is \
that there is some algebraic magice realting to cubes using the cube roots of \
unity.  Here it is:\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    \(\((x + \((\(-\(1\/2\)\) - \(I\ \@3\)\/2)\) y)\) 
        \((x + \((\(-\(1\/2\)\) + \(I\ \@3\)\/2)\) y)\) // Expand\n\), 
    \(\((x + \((\(-\(1\/2\)\) - \(I\ \@3\)\/2)\) y)\)\^3 + 
        \((x + \((\(-\(1\/2\)\) + \(I\ \@3\)\/2)\) y)\)\^3 // Expand\)}], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Now, the first product comes into the part of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(g\_5\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " that reads \n\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[
        \(\(-3\)\ x\^2\ z + 3\ x\ y\ z - 3\ y\^2\ z == \ 
          \(-3\) z \((x\^2 - x\ y + y\^2)\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ".\nThe second sum of two cubes stands (except for a change of sign) \
exactly in our polynomial.  This kind of \"factorization\" suggests we try \
another linear sumstitution (coming from a inverse of a matrix using the cube \
root of unity):"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g\_6 = 
      Tidy[g\_5 /. {
            x \[Rule] 
              \(-\((\(I + \@3\)\/\(2\ \@3\))\)\) x - 
                \((\(\(-I\) + \@3\)\/\(2\ \@3\))\)\ y, 
            y \[Rule] \(-\((I\/\@3)\)\) x - \((\(-\(I\/\@3\)\))\)\ y, 
            z \[Rule] z}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Wow!!! All those complex numbers get hidden under ",
  StyleBox["the rug of algebra",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "!   And, after a change of scale on ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["z",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", we get the third normal form as desired.  We need finally to check up on \
the double points."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[a, b, c, k]; 
    CL[PD[x\^3 + y\^3 + z\^3 - 3\ k\ x\ y\ z, \((a\ u + b\ v + c\ w)\)\^2]]
      \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note that if ",
  StyleBox["one",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\({a, b, c}\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " is ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["0",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ", they all are.  So take these three equations and eliminate two of those \
variables."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Eliminate[{a\^2 - b\ c\ k == 0, b\^2 - a\ c\ k == 0, 
        c\^2 - a\ b\ k == 0}, {b, c}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "For there to be a solution ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["k",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " must be ",
  StyleBox["a cube root of unity",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  Otherwise, the curve has no double point.  So we have done all that was \
required.  ",
  StyleBox["Q.E.D.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The configuration of inflection points ", "Subsection",
  Evaluatable->False,
  AspectRatioFixed->False],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Using a normal form", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Starting with the ",
  StyleBox["third normal form",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ", we try to find ",
  StyleBox["all",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " the inflection points of a cubic.  To do so we should look at the \
Hessian. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(ClearAll[f, g, h, a, b, c, Subscript]; 
    f = x\^3 + y\^3 + z\^3 - 3\ k\ x\ y\ z; \n
    g = Tidy/@Factor[Det[MHP[f]]]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "If ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(k == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " we have ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(x\ y\ z\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " (= three lines).  Otherwise, we can write a version of the Hessian in the \
form:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(h = x\^3 + y\^3 + z\^3 - \((4\/k\^2 - k)\)\ x\ y\ z\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This is just what we had before!  It will be ",
  StyleBox["singular",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if this vanishes:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(27 \( k\^6\) \((\((\((4\/k\^2 - k)\)/3)\)\^3 - 1)\) // Expand\)], 
  "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Let ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        StyleBox["j",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox["==",
          FontWeight->"Bold"], 
        StyleBox[
          SuperscriptBox[
            StyleBox["k",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "3"],
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]]],
  ", and solve for ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox["j",
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  ":"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[64 - 48\ j - 15\ j\^2 - j\^3 == 0, {j}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "We see we get a ",
  StyleBox["singular Hessian",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " if  ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(k\^3 == 1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " or ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(k\^3 == \(-8\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Be that as it may, the inflection points will be found at the \
intersection of the two curves.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(sol = Solve[{f == 0, h == 0, z == 1}, {x, y, z}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Ia it safe to algebraically simplify ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(\((1 - k\^3)\)\^\(1/3\)\/\((\(-1\) + k\^3)\)\^\(1/3\)\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " to just ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(-1\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "?  Well, let us try that."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(sol
        \_1 = {{x \[Rule] \(-1\), y \[Rule] 0, z \[Rule] 1}, {x \[Rule] 0, 
          y \[Rule] \(-1\), z \[Rule] 1}, {x \[Rule] 0, 
          y \[Rule] \((\(-1\))\)\^\(1/3\), z \[Rule] 1}, {x \[Rule] 0, 
          y \[Rule] \(-\((\(-1\))\)\^\(2/3\)\), z \[Rule] 1}, {
          x \[Rule] \((\(-1\))\)\^\(1/3\), y \[Rule] 0, z \[Rule] 1}, {
          x \[Rule] \(-\((\(-1\))\)\^\(2/3\)\), y \[Rule] 0, z \[Rule] 1}}; 
    \)\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Do these substitutions represent the intersections of the wo \
curves?\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({f, h} /. sol\_1\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "They do!  And what about the case when ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(k == 0\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  "?"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[{\((f /. k \[Rule] 0)\) == 0, x\ y\ z == 0, z == 1}, {x, y, z}]
      \)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Same solution!!!",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "  What we have proved is the following:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Theorem.",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  "  The configuration of inflection points of ",
  StyleBox["any",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " non-singular cubic is projectively equivalent the following ",
  StyleBox["fixed",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " configuration of nine points:\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{
        InterpretationBox[GridBox[{
              {\(\(-u\) + w\)},
              {\(\((1\/2 + \(I\ \@3\)\/2)\)\ u + w\)},
              {\(\((1\/2 - \(I\ \@3\)\/2)\)\ u + w\)},
              {\(\(-v\) + w\)},
              {\(\((1\/2 + \(I\ \@3\)\/2)\)\ v + w\)},
              {\(\((1\/2 - \(I\ \@3\)\/2)\)\ v + w\)},
              {\(\(-u\) + v\)},
              {\(\((1\/2 + \(I\ \@3\)\/2)\)\ u + v\)},
              {\(\((1\/2 - \(I\ \@3\)\/2)\)\ u + v\)}
              },
            RowSpacings->1,
            ColumnSpacings->3,
            RowAlignments->Baseline,
            ColumnAlignments->{Left}],
          TableForm[ {
            Plus[ 
              Times[ -1, u], w], 
            Plus[ 
              Times[ 
                Plus[ 
                  Rational[ 1, 2], 
                  Times[ 
                    Complex[ 0, 
                      Rational[ 1, 2]], 
                    Power[ 3, 
                      Rational[ 1, 2]]]], u], w], 
            Plus[ 
              Times[ 
                Plus[ 
                  Rational[ 1, 2], 
                  Times[ 
                    Complex[ 0, 
                      Rational[ -1, 2]], 
                    Power[ 3, 
                      Rational[ 1, 2]]]], u], w], 
            Plus[ 
              Times[ -1, v], w], 
            Plus[ 
              Times[ 
                Plus[ 
                  Rational[ 1, 2], 
                  Times[ 
                    Complex[ 0, 
                      Rational[ 1, 2]], 
                    Power[ 3, 
                      Rational[ 1, 2]]]], v], w], 
            Plus[ 
              Times[ 
                Plus[ 
                  Rational[ 1, 2], 
                  Times[ 
                    Complex[ 0, 
                      Rational[ -1, 2]], 
                    Power[ 3, 
                      Rational[ 1, 2]]]], v], w], 
            Plus[ 
              Times[ -1, u], v], 
            Plus[ 
              Times[ 
                Plus[ 
                  Rational[ 1, 2], 
                  Times[ 
                    Complex[ 0, 
                      Rational[ 1, 2]], 
                    Power[ 3, 
                      Rational[ 1, 2]]]], u], v], 
            Plus[ 
              Times[ 
                Plus[ 
                  Rational[ 1, 2], 
                  Times[ 
                    Complex[ 0, 
                      Rational[ -1, 2]], 
                    Power[ 3, 
                      Rational[ 1, 2]]]], u], v]}]], 
        StyleBox[" ",
          FontWeight->"Bold"]}]],
    FontWeight->"Bold"]
}], "Text",
  Background->RGBColor[0, 1, 1]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Note especially that this configuration is definitely in the ",
  StyleBox["complex plane",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  It is of a type that can be notated as",
  Cell[BoxData[
      RowBox[{"  ", 
        RowBox[{
          RowBox[{
            StyleBox[\({{9, 4}, {3, 12}}\),
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "\n", "      ", 
            StyleBox["\[UpArrow]",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["                     ",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], 
            StyleBox["\[UpArrow]",
              FontWeight->"Bold"], "\n", "    ", 
            StyleBox["points",
              FontSize->13,
              FontWeight->"Bold"]}], 
          StyleBox["           ",
            FontWeight->"Bold"], 
          StyleBox["lines",
            FontSize->13,
            FontWeight->"Bold"]}]}]]],
  ".  \nThis means that there ",
  StyleBox["9",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " distinct points and ",
  StyleBox["12",
    FontWeight->"Bold"],
  " lines; each of the points has exactly ",
  StyleBox["four",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the lines going through it; and each of the lines contains exactly ",
  StyleBox["three",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " of the points. Combinatorially, this is similar to taking the rows, \
columns, and both sets of diagonals of a ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      StyleBox[\(3\[Times]3\),
        FontWeight->"Bold"]]],
  " matrix of points as showing the lines.  It can be argued that this \
configuration does not exist in the ",
  StyleBox["real plane",
    FontWeight->"Bold",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ".  (There is a brief proof in the book by Veblen and Young, p. 249.)  "
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]]
},
FrontEndVersion->"Macintosh 3.0",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 800}, {0, 580}},
